[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Row Versioning and Snapshot Isolation in Microsoft SQL Server"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Traditional locking mechanisms in SQL Server can cause concurrency bottlenecks when readers block writers and writers block readers. Row versioning and snapshot isolation provide an alternative concurrency model that improves application scalability by maintaining past versions of rows in tempdb."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding How Row Versioning Works"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When row versioning is enabled, SQL Server maintains a historical record of data modifications. Whenever a row is updated or deleted, the Database Engine stores the previous version of the row in the tempdb database. Each row version is tagged with a transaction sequence number that identifies the point in time the version was created."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Readers do not block writers: Queries reading data do not acquire shared locks, allowing concurrent updates to proceed without waiting."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Writers do not block readers: Modifying transactions do not block read operations because readers access the row versions in tempdb rather than the current locked rows."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Enabling Snapshot Isolation"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before you can use snapshot isolation transactions, you must explicitly enable the ALLOW_SNAPSHOT_ISOLATION database option on the target database."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"ALTER DATABASE YourDatabaseName SET ALLOW_SNAPSHOT_ISOLATION ON;"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Snapshot Isolation in Transactions"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once enabled at the database level, applications can initiate a snapshot transaction using the SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL command. Queries within this transaction will see a consistent snapshot of the database as it existed at the moment the transaction started."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SNAPSHOT;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

SELECT EmployeeID, Salary 
FROM Employees 
WHERE Department = 'Engineering';

COMMIT TRANSACTION;"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Read-Committed Snapshot Isolation (RCSI)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Instead of requiring application code changes to use explicit snapshot isolation transactions, you can enable Read-Committed Snapshot Isolation (RCSI). This setting alters the default READ COMMITTED isolation level to use row versioning, ensuring that standard read queries do not acquire shared locks."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"ALTER DATABASE YourDatabaseName SET READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT ON;"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Monitoring and Best Practices"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because row versioning heavily utilizes tempdb, you must monitor tempdb space usage and ensure adequate IO subsystem performance. Long-running snapshot transactions can cause row versions to accumulate in tempdb, preventing cleanup and leading to potential storage exhaustion."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep snapshot transactions as short as possible to allow timely cleanup of tempdb version store."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor the 'Version Store Size (KB)' and 'Free Space in tempdb (KB)' performance counters."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provision tempdb across multiple data files and fast storage disks to avoid allocation contention."}]
